LESSONS_I6 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The branch network: scope and model", "11", "<p>This module covers multi-site stock operations in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: operate the hub-and-spoke replenishment model with Material Requests and reorder levels; run the weekly replenishment cycle; allocate stock under scarcity by written rule; open a new branch's stock by transfer with the tight first cycle; run laterals and enforce the purchasing boundary; and read the branch network's standing reports.</p><p><b>The model, stated once.</b> Modules 1-5 built the machinery: warehouses with custody, transfers through transit, identity on movements, counts proving custody, value following documents. This module is that machinery operating as a network. The standing design from module 1's worked example:</p><p>1. <b>Central buys.</b> Procurement concentrates at the hub — supplier terms, receiving discipline, landed costs, and the quality gate all live once, at Central.<br>2. <b>Branches sell and request.</b> A branch's stock arrives by transfer from Central; the branch's job is selling, counting, and requesting — not supplier management.<br>3. <b>Everything moves by the modules already built:</b> requests as documents, despatches through transit (module 2), identity travelling (module 3), receipts counted against despatches, value carried by the ledger (module 5).</p><p><b>Why the boundary matters.</b> The branch that buys locally outside the lanes acquires stock at unmatched prices with no receiving control, no landed costs, no batch capture, and no supplier accountability — five modules of discipline bypassed in one purchase. Chapter 6 covers the exception lane for the genuine emergency; the boundary itself is policy, written in module 1 and enforced here.</p><p><b>Custody at network scale.</b> Each site's keeper answers for their warehouses (module 1); the stock manager at Central owns the network flows — the replenishment run, the allocation decisions, the lateral approvals; the reads (Chapter 7) are how the center answers for stock it cannot see from a desk. The division is the programme's standing shape: the sites execute and attest locally, the center decides and reads network-wide, and every flow between them is a document.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the request machinery — Material Requests and reorder levels. Chapter 3: the weekly replenishment run. Chapter 4: allocation under scarcity. Chapter 5: opening a new branch. Chapter 6: laterals, returns, and the purchasing boundary. Chapter 7: the branch reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The request machinery", "12", "<p>Branch demand reaches Central as documents. Two instruments: the Material Request, and the reorder levels that raise requests automatically.</p><p><b>1. The Material Request.</b> Open Material Request (awesome bar) &gt; New:</p><p>1. <b>Purpose: Material Transfer</b> — the branch's ask for stock from another warehouse (the purchase-purpose requests belong to the Procurement track; the transfer purpose is this module's instrument).<br>2. <b>The rows</b> — item, quantity, the requesting warehouse (Set From/Target per the form: the branch's stock room as the destination), and the required-by date.<br>3. <b>Submission</b> — the request becomes a visible, countable demand: the replenishment run works from submitted requests, the Ordered/Requested figures feed projected quantities (module 1, Chapter 6), and the request's fulfilment status tracks what the despatches delivered against it.</p><p><b>2. Reorder levels — the automatic ask.</b> On the Item record, the reorder table carries one row per warehouse:</p><p>1. <b>Warehouse</b> — the branch stock room the rule watches.<br>2. <b>Re-order Level</b> — the projected-quantity threshold: when the warehouse's projected quantity falls below it, the system raises a Material Request automatically.<br>3. <b>Re-order Qty</b> — the quantity the raised request asks for.<br>4. <b>Material Request Type: Transfer</b> — the branch rows request transfer from the hub; purchase-type rows belong at Central where buying happens.</p><p><b>3. Setting the levels.</b> The arithmetic is written, per item per branch, from the sell-through data (Chapter 7): the level covers expected sales across the replenishment interval plus the transit lead time, with the buffer the policy sets per item class (essential lines carry larger buffers; module 7's classifications feed this). The levels are reviewed quarterly against actual sell-through — a level set once and never revisited drifts into stockouts on rising lines and overstock on falling ones, which is exactly the pattern the quarterly review reads for.</p><p><b>4. The request disciplines.</b></p><p>1. <b>Projected, not actual:</b> the trigger and the manual request alike reason on projected quantity — stock on the shelf minus commitments plus what is already coming. A branch that requests on shelf-count alone double-orders what is already in transit to it.<br>2. <b>Requests are honest asks:</b> the branch requests what the data supports, not a padded figure hedging against short allocation — Chapter 4's allocation rules exist precisely so padding is unnecessary, and the run compares requests against sell-through (Chapter 3) so padding is also visible.<br>3. <b>One request per branch per cycle</b> as the standing rhythm (plus the exception request for the genuine urgent case, flagged as such): the run works a predictable queue, and the branch consolidates its week's needs into one reviewable document.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The weekly replenishment run", "12", "<p>The network breathes on a cycle. This chapter is the weekly procedure, step by step, as the stock manager runs it.</p><p><b>1. The inputs, assembled (thirty minutes).</b></p><p>1. The submitted Material Requests — the branches' consolidated asks plus any auto-raised reorder requests since last run.<br>2. The sell-through view (Chapter 7) — each branch's recent movement on the requested lines.<br>3. The hub's availability — Central's projected quantities on the requested items.<br>4. The transit state — module 2's read: what is already moving, so the run does not re-send what is on the road.</p><p><b>2. The review pass.</b> Per request line: the ask against the branch's sell-through and current projected quantity. Three outcomes:</p><p>1. <b>Approved as asked</b> — the data supports it.<br>2. <b>Adjusted with a note</b> — the ask disagrees with the data (the padded line, the forgotten in-transit quantity); adjusted quantities carry one-line reasons back to the branch, because a silent cut teaches padding and a reasoned one teaches the arithmetic.<br>3. <b>Held for the scarcity procedure</b> — total asks exceed hub availability on a line: Chapter 4 takes over for that line.</p><p><b>3. The despatch pass.</b> The approved quantities become the pick list per branch; module 2's machinery executes:</p><p>1. One despatch entry per vehicle-load per branch (Add to Transit), submitted as the truck loads, batches on the rows (module 3).<br>2. The despatch references the Material Requests it fulfils — the request's status tracks fulfilment, and the branch sees what is coming against what it asked.<br>3. The branch receives against the despatch rows — identity and quantity confirmed, shortfalls per module 2's procedure.</p><p><b>4. The run's closing note.</b> Ten lines, filed weekly: requests received and their total lines, adjustments made with reasons, scarcity lines and their allocations (Chapter 4), despatches submitted with references, and the exceptions carried to next run. The note is the run's audit trail and the new stock manager's training file — the run must be a procedure a competent person can take over from the file, per the programme's handover standard.</p><p><b>5. The cadence decision.</b> Weekly is the standing default; the policy sets it per the business's rhythm. The rule that governs any cadence: the replenishment interval plus transit lead time is the window the reorder arithmetic covers (Chapter 2) — shortening the cycle without recomputing levels overstocks the branches; lengthening it without recomputing understocks them. Cycle and levels move together or not at all.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Allocation under scarcity", "12", "<p>When the hub cannot fill every approved ask, allocation becomes a decision. The rule of this chapter: scarcity is allocated by written rule, documented, and communicated — never by who asked loudest or last.</p><p><b>1. When the procedure triggers.</b> The run's review pass (Chapter 3) finds a line where the branches' approved asks total more than Central's available quantity — the supplier short-shipped, demand spiked, or an import is late. The line moves from the despatch pass into this procedure.</p><p><b>2. The written allocation rules.</b> The policy page, agreed with the owner before the first scarce week, in application order:</p><p>1. <b>The essential floor first.</b> For items the policy marks essential (the classification from module 7's data plus the business's judgment — for a pharmaceutical network, the critical-care lines), every stocking branch receives the floor quantity before anything else allocates. The floor is a written per-item figure, sized to the minimum service level the business will not breach at any branch.<br>2. <b>Pro-rata by sell-through above the floor.</b> The remaining quantity divides across branches in proportion to their recent sell-through on the line (Chapter 7's eight-week read) — the stock goes where it is provably selling, by arithmetic anyone can check.<br>3. <b>Rounding to shippable units</b> — cartons round per the policy's rule, differences carried to next cycle rather than argued.</p><p><b>3. The documentation.</b> The allocation worksheet files with the run's note: the line, the available quantity, each branch's ask and sell-through figure, the floor applied, the pro-rata arithmetic, and the allocated result. The worksheet is the answer to every version of the question one branch will ask — why did the other site get more — and the answer is a calculation, not a conversation.</p><p><b>4. The communication.</b> Each affected branch receives, with its despatch advice: the allocated quantity against its ask, the one-line reason (short supply; floor plus pro-rata applied), and the expected relief date if known (the purchase order's schedule from the Procurement track). Branches that understand the rule stop padding requests (Chapter 2's discipline holding), because padding cannot move a pro-rata allocation built on sell-through data — the arithmetic ignores the ask's size beyond eligibility.</p><p><b>5. The scarcity aftermath.</b> Two follow-ups close the loop:</p><p>1. <b>The relief despatch</b> — when supply lands, the shorted branches are made whole first, from the worksheet's carried differences, before the normal cycle resumes.<br>2. <b>The pattern check</b> — a line hitting the scarcity procedure repeatedly is a purchasing signal, not an allocation problem: the reorder arithmetic at the hub (Central's own levels against total network demand) goes to the procurement owner with the worksheets as data.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Opening a new branch", "12", "<p>A new branch's stock is a project with a checklist. This chapter is the procedure, and its two governing rules: <b>the branch opens by transfer, never by reconciliation</b>, and <b>opening estimates are always wrong — so the first cycle reads tight.</b></p><p><b>1. The structural prerequisites (module 1's work).</b> Before any stock moves: the site's warehouses created in the tree per the convention (the stock room, and the specials the site needs); the keeper and deputy named on the custody list; user permissions scoping the branch team; the POS profile's warehouse pointing at the branch stock room (the ZhiftPOS track's commissioning); the Item Defaults reviewed for the site where the wiring convention requires it.</p><p><b>2. The opening range and quantities.</b> The range list builds from the nearest comparable branch's data — its stocking list and sell-through, adjusted for the new site's expected profile. The honesty rule is stated in the plan itself: these figures are estimates, they will be wrong item by item, and the correction mechanism is the tight first cycle (step 4), not debate over the estimates.</p><p><b>3. The opening transfer.</b> The opening stock despatches from Central as ordinary two-step transfers (module 2): despatch entries with batches on the rows, transit legs, the branch receiving against despatch rows with full counting discipline. Why never a reconciliation: an opening reconciliation types quantities and rates into existence with no document trail behind them (module 5's opening-rate hazards, recreated voluntarily at every branch), while a transfer moves documented, valued, identified stock — the bins open true, the batches arrive with their expiries, the values carry from the ledger, and day one's records are as trustworthy as Central's. The reconciliation instrument at a branch opening has exactly one honest use: nothing (the go-live Opening Stock purpose belongs to the company's original implementation, not to branch expansion).</p><p><b>4. The first ninety days — the tight cycle.</b> Because the opening estimates are wrong by construction:</p><p>1. <b>Weekly sell-through review, line by line</b> — the new branch's movement against the opening assumptions, read every week for the first ninety days (against the standing network's routine cadence).<br>2. <b>Replenishment on the data, not the plan</b> — the weekly run treats the new branch's requests with fresh eyes: the fast movers identified and levels set from actual sales; the dead openers flagged early for lateral return (Chapter 6) before they age into module 7's problem lists.<br>3. <b>Reorder levels written at day sixty</b> — enough data to set the Chapter 2 arithmetic honestly; the automatic machinery takes over from manual review as the levels prove out.<br>4. <b>The first cycle count inside month one</b> — module 4's programme starts immediately at the new site; the first month's count closes the branch's bins against physical truth while every discrepancy is young and traceable.</p><p><b>5. The opening file.</b> The checklist, the range list with its source data, the opening despatch references, the weekly review notes, and the day-sixty level decisions — one folder, the branch's stock history from its first carton, per the standing one-lookup standard.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Laterals, returns, and the purchasing boundary", "12", "<p>Three flows complete the network's movement set: branch-to-branch laterals, returns to the hub, and the exception lane at the purchasing boundary.</p><p><b>1. Laterals — branch to branch.</b> The case: one branch overstocks what another sells (the rebalancing read, Chapter 7, or a scarcity relief). The rules:</p><p>1. <b>Approved at the center</b> — laterals are proposed by the read or the branches but approved by the stock manager, because the network view (what is in transit, what the run is about to despatch) lives at Central and a well-meant lateral can cross a replenishment already moving.<br>2. <b>Executed as standard transfers</b> — two-step through transit where sites are apart (module 2), identity on the rows (module 3), received against despatch. A lateral is not an informal favour between branch managers; it is a transfer with every discipline attached.<br>3. <b>Recorded with its reason</b> — the lateral's note names the read or decision behind it; the quarterly lateral census (Chapter 7) reads the pattern, because recurring laterals on a line mean the allocation or the levels are wrong at the source.</p><p><b>2. Returns to the hub.</b> Slow stock, near-expiry lines moving to where they sell (module 3's rebalancing lane), range delistings, and recalled goods all flow branch-to-Central as transfers with their reasons. The receiving discipline holds in reverse: Central counts the return against the despatch, and near-expiry returns land per module 3's lanes rather than disappearing into the main store.</p><p><b>3. The purchasing boundary and its exception lane.</b> The policy from module 1: branches do not buy. The exception lane, for the genuine case (the emergency purchase the service level requires and Central cannot despatch in time):</p><p>1. <b>Pre-approved in writing</b> — the branch requests, the stock manager or owner approves per the threshold, before the purchase.<br>2. <b>Executed through the purchasing documents</b> — the Procurement track's flow at branch scale: a real order, a real receipt at the branch's warehouse, the invoice matched. The exception uses the lanes at small scale; it never bypasses them.<br>3. <b>Listed monthly</b> — every exception purchase on one list to the owner with reasons. The list's healthy state is nearly empty; a branch appearing repeatedly is a replenishment failure to fix at the run (the levels, the cadence, the transit time), not a boundary to keep waiving — the exception that becomes a habit is the boundary dissolving in slow motion.</p><p><b>4. What never happens, restated.</b> Stock does not move between branches on a phone call; purchases do not happen on a branch manager's card with the paperwork to follow; returns do not accumulate in a back room awaiting a quiet month. Every flow in this chapter is the standard machinery with a reason attached — the network has no informal lanes.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The branch reads", "12", "<p>The center answers for stock it cannot see. These reads are how. Each is stated with its cadence, its source, and its action.</p><p><b>1. The sell-through read — weekly, per branch.</b> The eight-week movement view per item per branch (the sales and issue history from the ledger, compiled by the standing report): what each branch actually sells, trended. This read is the module's workhorse — it feeds the replenishment review (Chapter 3), the allocation arithmetic (Chapter 4), the new-branch cycle (Chapter 5), and the rebalancing proposals below. The sell-through percentage per line (units sold against units stocked across the window) is the comparable figure: a line at 58% at Ibadan and 12% at Akure is a rebalancing candidate readable in one row.</p><p><b>2. The branch stock page — monthly, to the owner.</b> One page per branch with the close: stock value (the Stock Balance at the site's node — module 1's tree paying off), the value trend, the site's count accuracy (module 4's metric per warehouse), open transit lines, and the month's exceptions (shortfalls, exception purchases, laterals). The page is the branch's stock health in five figures, comparable across sites and months.</p><p><b>3. The rebalancing read — monthly.</b> The sell-through comparison across branches on common lines: overstocked-here-selling-there candidates, proposed as laterals with the arithmetic attached (Chapter 6's approval lane). The read proposes; the stock manager disposes — and the standing preference order is levels-fix first, lateral second: a lateral corrects a symptom once, the level correction stops it recurring.</p><p><b>4. The network accuracy comparison — monthly.</b> Module 4's accuracy metric side by side across sites. The trailing site gets the next spot count and the process review as data — the same read the counting module defined, operated at network scale.</p><p><b>5. The transit and boundary reads — weekly.</b> Module 2's transit age read run network-wide (every aged line named to its despatch), and the monthly exception-purchase list (Chapter 6). Both are boundary-integrity reads: the network's rules hold exactly as well as these lists stay short.</p><p><b>6. The reorder health read — quarterly.</b> Chapter 2's levels against the quarter's actual sell-through, per branch: lines whose levels triggered stockouts (level too low or transit slower than assumed), lines that never triggered while stock aged (level too high or line dying — module 7's territory adjoins here). The read closes with level corrections written and dated, which is what keeps the automatic machinery calibrated to the business the branches actually do.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: HealthTrade opens Akure, and the Emzor short-close", "13", "<p>This chapter runs Chapters 2-7 through two network events. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Event 1 — opening Akure (Chapter 5).</b></p><p>1. <b>Prerequisites:</b> the Akure warehouses stand in the tree from module 1's design; the keeper and deputy named; permissions scoped; the POS profile pointed at <i>Akure - Stock Room</i>.<br>2. <b>The range:</b> built from Ibadan's stocking list and sell-through as the comparable, trimmed for the smaller catchment — with the plan stating the honesty rule: these are estimates and the first cycle will correct them.<br>3. <b>The opening transfer:</b> despatched from Central as standard two-step transfers, batches on every row, received at Akure against despatch rows. <b>Opening by transfer, never reconciliation</b> — Akure's bins open documented, valued, and identified from the first carton.<br>4. <b>The tight cycle:</b> weekly line-by-line sell-through review for <b>ninety days</b>. Week three already shows the pattern the plan predicted it could not predict: four lines selling at twice the estimate, a tail of slow openers flagged early. Replenishment follows the data; two dead openers return to Central by lateral in week six before aging into a problem list; reorder levels write at day sixty from actual sales. The first cycle count runs inside month one and <b>month one closes with Akure's bins true</b> — the opening file holding the checklist, despatch references, weekly notes, and level decisions.</p><p><b>Event 2 — the Emzor short-close (Chapter 4).</b> A supplier short-ships a fast Emzor line into a demand spike: the weekly run finds branch asks totalling <b>300 cartons against 180 available</b> across the three sites. The line moves to the allocation procedure:</p><p>1. <b>The essential floor first:</b> the line is on the essential list; the written floor of <b>30 cartons per stocking branch</b> allocates 90 across the three sites before anything else.<br>2. <b>Pro-rata by sell-through:</b> the remaining 90 divide in proportion to each branch's eight-week sell-through on the line — the arithmetic on the worksheet, checkable by anyone.<br>3. <b>Documented and communicated:</b> the worksheet files with the run's note; each branch receives its allocation with the one-line reason and the relief date from the purchase order's schedule. The question one branch manager asks — why did Ibadan get more — is answered by the worksheet's sell-through column in one look.<br>4. <b>The aftermath:</b> the relief despatch makes the shorted branches whole first when supply lands the following week; the pattern check finds this the line's first scarcity event in the year — no purchasing signal, the worksheet files, the cycle resumes.</p><p><b>The month's network page.</b> The branch pages to the MD show: Akure's opening month with bins true and its first accuracy figure; Ibadan's <b>58% sell-through</b> on the watched line against Akure's early figures feeding the rebalancing read; transit at zero aged lines through both events; the exception-purchase list empty — the boundary holding through a scarce month, which is when boundaries are actually tested.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in network operations, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — requests from the shelf, not the projection.</b> Symptom: branches double-ordering what is already in transit; overstock arriving in waves. Correction: the affected lines rebalanced; the request discipline re-briefed. Prevention: requests and reorder triggers reasoning on projected quantity — shelf minus commitments plus incoming.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the padded ask.</b> Symptom: requests inflated to hedge against short allocation; the run's data polluted; trust eroding both directions. Correction: adjustments with reasons; the allocation rules communicated. Prevention: Chapter 4's arithmetic — pro-rata on sell-through ignores the ask's size, so padding buys nothing and shows in the review.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the branch opened by reconciliation.</b> Symptom: opening quantities and rates typed into existence; no document trail; module 5's opening hazards recreated at branch scale. Correction: where recent, the reconciliation reversed and the opening re-run by transfer; where aged, the valuation review with the accountant. Prevention: the rule — branches open by transfer, and the Opening Stock purpose belongs to the original implementation only.</p><p><b>Error 4 — estimates defended instead of corrected.</b> Symptom: the new branch replenished to the plan for months; fast lines stocked out while dead openers age. Correction: the tight cycle started late — weekly reads, levels from actuals. Prevention: the honesty rule written into the opening plan: estimates are wrong by construction, and the first ninety days read weekly.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the informal lateral.</b> Symptom: stock moving between branches on phone calls; transfers crossing replenishments already in transit; the center's network view wrong. Correction: the movements documented; the approval lane restored. Prevention: laterals approved at Central and executed as standard transfers with reasons — no informal lanes.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the exception that became a habit.</b> Symptom: one branch's emergency purchases recurring monthly; the boundary dissolving in slow motion. Correction: the replenishment failure fixed at its source — the levels, the cadence, or the transit assumption. Prevention: the monthly exception list to the owner, read as a replenishment health report rather than a permissions log.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the hub-and-spoke model and why the purchasing boundary exists; raise and review Material Requests and set reorder rows with the projected-quantity arithmetic; run the weekly cycle — inputs, review pass with reasoned adjustments, despatch pass, closing note; allocate scarcity by the written rules — floor first, pro-rata by sell-through, documented and communicated, with the relief and pattern follow-ups; open a branch by transfer with the ninety-day tight cycle and day-sixty levels; operate laterals, returns, and the exception lane; and run the six branch reads on their cadences.</p><p><b>Module rule 6: the network replenishes by procedure — requests reasoned on projected quantities, a weekly run with reasoned adjustments, scarcity allocated by written rule and shown as arithmetic, branches opened by transfer and read tight for ninety days, and every flow through the standard machinery with a reason attached.</b> Module 7 covers stock health and analytics.</p>"),
]

